speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, by the courtesy of the member for South Lanark (Hon. Mr. Haggart), who had the floor when the House rose for dinner, I desire to make a few remarks in reply to the hon. Minister of Customs in reference to this steel rail deal. But first I desire to reply to the hon. Finance Minister, who took me very severely to task for my audacity in daring to do what he calls lecturing a minister of the Crown. I take it, Sir, that a representative of the people has a perfect right, so long as he keeps within the rules of this House, to express his opinion even of a minister of the Crown and his conduct of public business. I hope I know how to draw the line between speaking of a minister of the Crown in his public capacity and making any allusions to his private character or conduct. I speak with the greatest def erence, being a new member. Now, the hon. Minister of Customs, a gentleman for whom I have great respect as Minister of Cu'stoms, made some very misleading statements on this question, and he must have known them to be misleading. He stated that the starting of this steel rail industry was secured without any cost to this country. I make the statement that the cost to this country is an absolute cost which we can easily gauge. It is a cost of $3 a ton on the pig iron, and $3 a ton on the steel rails, making in all a bounty of $6 a ton, which on 25,000 tons a year amounts to $150,000 a year, which must be paid out of the revenues of this country, which revenues come out of the people of this country. Therefore, when the Minister of Customs states that this industry Is established without any cost to this country, he makes a statement which he cannot substantiate, and which he must have known to be incorrect. The hon. Minister 166}
of Railways stated that the government were protecting this poor company, who are doing so much to supply us with steel rails, to the extent of $6 a ton, which he agreed to pay them over the market price at the time he made the contract, because he wished to protect them against the slaughter prices in the United States. I contend that if the bounty which this company is getting was not sufficient, the government should have made it sufficient, so that they would have been able to compete with any steel rail producers on the face of the earth. I maintain that it is fair competition they would receive from the steel rail producers of the United States.
They say that this industry should not be subjected to competition against slaughter prices. Well, I would like them to be consistent. Not only' would I like the gentlemen on the Treasury benches to be consistent, but also those sitting behind them, who are prepared, as free traders, to shout for free trade, but who, as supporters of the government, vote for protection whenever the government bold up their hands. If they have any regard at all for consistency', there is an industry which was established and has grown up under protection, and from whom a portion of that protection they have taken away; and if they have any' regard for consistency, they ought to restore that industry to its former position. Have they ever inquired whether the great woollen industry has not to tight against slaughter prices ? I contend that there has been an exceptional slaughter of prices in England this year, such as never has taken place before; yet in face of that, they have given British goods a preference in this market without obtaining for us a corresponding return. The woollen manufacturers of Yorkshire have turned out more shoddy goods-many the production of Germany and other countries-then ever before, and have brought them in here to be sacrificed at slaughter prices, and those gentlemen, who want to protect this steel rail company against slaughter prices in steel rails, should have some little consideration for the thousands of woollen manufactures in this country and the millions of dollars invested in that industry. There is not a woollen mill in this country to-day paying expenses. I am connected with several, and I handed the hon. Finance Minister to-day a statement, which I am prepared to stand by, which shows that the statement he made that these mills were all making money was not correct.
